+        "jsCode": "// 25_ A.3 — owner AUTHORSHIP and external CONTENT are two trust cases and must\n// not share one class.\n//\n// What changed at R3, and why it matters: authority no longer derives from any\n// field this workflow can set. Rev 4 let an `origin: 'owner'` flag do it, which\n// meant a provider could author content and have it recorded as the owner's.\n// Authority now derives ONLY from an `idea_ref` the owner minted at\n// /owner/idea, against content the wrapper stored server-side. Anything this\n// workflow sends as `owner_content` is a convenience copy: it must canonicalize\n// to the stored digest or the request is refused, and it NEVER replaces the\n// stored content.\n//\n// Every external item carries its OWN envelope. An item never inherits\n// authority from the enclosing idea — quoting something does not authorise it.\nreturn items.map(i => {\n  const ideaRef = i.json.idea_ref || null;          // owner-minted, or absent\n  const externals = (i.json.external_items || []).map(it => ({\n    envelope: { partition: 'business', trust: 'external-untrusted' },\n    source: it.source || 'unknown',\n    content: it.content\n  }));\n  const out = { external_items: externals };\n  if (ideaRef) {\n    // Case A. The ref is the authority; the copy is only corroborating.\n    out.idea_ref = ideaRef;\n    if (i.json.owner_content) out.owner_content = i.json.owner_content;\n  } else {\n    // Case B. No owner authority at all; the top-level envelope is required.\n    out.envelope = { partition: 'business', trust: 'external-untrusted' };\n  }\n  return { json: out };\n});"
